1. Purpose

Test subcycle time stepping

2. Grid functions

"iteration" is stamped with 100 + 10*cctk_iteration + cctk_level at INITIAL
and at EVOL, so its value on each level records the last pass on which that
level was evolved.

"postrestrict" (restrict="no") is stamped the same way at INITIAL and at
CCTK_POSTRESTRICT. Restriction never overwrites it, so its value on each
level records the last pass on which that level was inside the active range
of a CCTK_POSTRESTRICT traversal. The driver only enters the restriction
phase when the finest level has caught up with its parent, so with 2 levels
and subcycling the level-1 stamp is unchanged on odd passes (1, 3, ...) and
both levels update on even passes (2, 4, ...). If the restriction phase ever
runs on a pass where nothing is time-aligned, the finest level's stamp
changes on an odd pass and the test fails.

With 3 levels (test/subcycle_3levels.par, two coarse steps) the finest level
aligns with level 1 on every second pass and with level 0 on every fourth,
so within each coarse step the "postrestrict" stamps update as follows:

  pass 1: none          (levels at t = 1, 1/2, 1/4)
  pass 2: levels 1, 2   (levels at t = 1, 1/2, 1/2; level 2 restricted into 1)
  pass 3: none          (levels at t = 1, 1, 3/4)
  pass 4: levels 0,1,2  (levels at t = 1, 1, 1; full cascade)

Pass 2 is the case a too-eager gate would get wrong: level 0 must keep its
previous stamp while levels 1 and 2 update.

"prerestrict" (restrict="no") is stamped the same way at INITIAL and in the
driver's CarpetX_PreRestrict group, which CarpetX traverses before the
reflux and the restriction, over the same widened level range as
CCTK_POSTRESTRICT. Its stamps must therefore match "postrestrict" on every
pass; a difference means the hook and the restriction have drifted apart.

"tail_context" (checkpoint="no") holds on each level the context of the last
CCTK_POSTSTEP traversal whose window covered the level: tail_iteration,
tail_time and tail_timefac are that traversal's cctk_iteration, cctk_time and
cctk_timefac. It is stamped everywhere, so it needs no SYNC, and after a
restart it is also stamped in CCTK_POST_RECOVER_VARIABLES. Under subcycling
CCTK_POSTSTEP (and CCTK_ANALYSIS) runs once per pass, after the batch that
steps the finest level and after the restriction: its window is the finest
level, widened down to every coarser level that now shares its clock, i.e.
the levels this pass finalized. It sees the finest level's context:
cctk_time = cctk_delta_time * the finest clock and cctk_timefac = 2^(finest
level). A coarser level that steps ahead of the finest level gets no
end-of-step window until the finest level catches up with it. With 3 levels
(test/subcycle_3levels.par, where cctk_delta_time = 1) the stamps
(iteration, time, timefac) after each pass of the first coarse step are:

  pass 1: window [2,3)   L0 (0,0,4)  L1 (0,0,4)    L2 (1,1/4,4)
  pass 2: window [1,3)   L0 (0,0,4)  L1 (2,1/2,4)  L2 (2,1/2,4)
  pass 3: window [2,3)   L0 (0,0,4)  L1 (2,1/2,4)  L2 (3,3/4,4)
  pass 4: window [0,3)   L0 (4,1,4)  L1 (4,1,4)    L2 (4,1,4)

The second coarse step repeats this with iterations 5 to 8 and times larger
by 1. Initialization runs CCTK_POSTSTEP once over every level, with the
finest level's context as at every aligned pass, so it stamps every level
with (0,0,4). A level keeps its stamp until it is finalized, i.e. until a
later window covers it: level 0 keeps (0,0,4) through pass 3, and level 1
keeps (2,1/2,4) through pass 3. Each level's stamp changes once per step of
that level, and its time never repeats.

After a restart, recovery traverses CCTK_POST_RECOVER_VARIABLES once per
clock group (a run of adjacent levels that share a clock), coarse to fine.
Each traversal covers the group's levels and sees the context in which they
last stepped: the widened window after the group's finest level last
stepped, with that level's context. The finest clock group shares the
finest level's clock, so the last pass also finalized it, and it holds the
stamp the uninterrupted run holds after the checkpointed pass. Every coarser
group is provisional: it has stepped ahead of the finest level but has not
been finalized at its clock yet, so after recovery it holds its last-step
context while the uninterrupted run still holds the stamp of its last
finalization. The two agree again from the first window that covers the
group. Restarting the 3-level run from each pass of the second coarse step
gives these traversals, each with its window and its (iteration, time,
timefac):

  from iteration 5 (clocks 2, 3/2, 5/4): [0,1) (5,2,1)  [1,2) (5,3/2,2)  [2,3) (5,5/4,4)
  from iteration 6 (clocks 2, 3/2, 3/2): [0,1) (5,2,1)  [1,3) (6,3/2,4)
  from iteration 7 (clocks 2, 2, 7/4):   [0,2) (7,2,2)  [2,3) (7,7/4,4)
  from iteration 8 (clocks 2, 2, 2):     [0,3) (8,2,4)

Only the last group of each row is finalized; e.g. from iteration 5 the
uninterrupted run holds (4,1,4) on levels 0 and 1 until they are finalized
at iterations 8 and 6.

The recover tests in TestSubcyclingMC2/test,
recover-subcycling-openpmd-3clocks.par and -4clocks.par, output such stamps
after restarts from iterations 5 and 9. Their references equal the
uninterrupted run's (checkpoint-subcycling-openpmd-3clocks and -4clocks)
except on provisional levels before their next finalization: level 0 at
iteration 6 (3 clocks) and 12 (4 clocks) holds its recovery stamp, (5,2,1)
and (9,2,1) in units of cctk_delta_time, where the uninterrupted run holds
(4,1,4) and (8,1,8). TestSubcyclingMC2's error, recomputed in the same
traversal, differs on the same rows. recover-subcycling-openpmd-addlevel.par
does the same after a restart from iteration 6, right after a regrid added a
fourth level (clocks 2, 3/2, 3/2, 11/8): level 0 last stepped in iteration
5, before level 3 existed, so its traversal [0,1) sees iteration 5, where
the clocks alone would give 4; it holds (5,2,1) at iterations 7 and 8, where
the uninterrupted run holds (4,1,4). recover-subcycling-silo-3clocks.par
repeats the 3-clock restart from a Silo checkpoint, in relaxed recovery
mode; its references equal those of recover-subcycling-openpmd-3clocks.
recover-subcycling-openpmd-iteration0.par restarts from the initial
checkpoint (IO::checkpoint_ID), where every clock is 0: one traversal [0,3)
(0,0,4), the initialization stamp, and its references, iteration 0
included, equal the uninterrupted run's
(checkpoint-subcycling-openpmd-iteration0) except for recovery_counts.
Iteration 1's tail_context and error are not compared in this test. The
checkpoint was written on 1 process; recovered on 2, the regrid at the top
of iteration 1 splits each level into two boxes and remakes levels 1 and 2,
and a remake does not carry groups that are not checkpointed. Level 1 then
steps ahead of the finest level, so its tail_context and error stay
poisoned until level 1 is finalized at iteration 2, while on 1 process (and
in the uninterrupted run) nothing is remade and level 1 still holds
(0,0,4). The reference therefore omits those two variables at iteration 1;
the state at iteration 1, and every variable from iteration 2 on, are
compared as usual.

"recovery_counts" (grid scalars, checkpoint="no") count the traversals of
CCTK_POST_RECOVER_VARIABLES (count_post_recover), CCTK_POSTSTEP
(count_poststep), CCTK_ANALYSIS (count_analysis), CarpetX_PreRestrict
(count_prerestrict) and CCTK_POSTRESTRICT (count_postrestrict) during
recovery. CCTK_RECOVER_VARIABLES zeroes them and turns counting on, and
CCTK_CPINITIAL turns it off, so the first output after a restart shows which
bins recovery ran: count_post_recover equals the number of clock groups and
the other counts are 0, since recovery does not rerun end-of-step bins. A
fresh start zeroes them at CCTK_INITIAL with counting off, so they stay 0.
The recover tests in TestSubcyclingMC2/test output them:
recover-subcycling-openpmd-nonrhs.par (two clock groups), -3clocks.par
(three), -4clocks.par (four), -addlevel.par (three), -iteration0.par (one)
and recover-subcycling-silo-3clocks.par (three).

3. The end-of-step sync fixture

Under subcycling the driver aborts on a SYNC requested while it traverses
CCTK_POSTSTEP, CCTK_ANALYSIS, CCTK_CHECKPOINT or CCTK_TERMINATE on a window
that does not hold every level, since the sync would fill the window's
coarsest level from the provisional level below it (see "End-of-step bins
under subcycling" in the CarpetX documentation). Every test above shows that
nothing in these bins syncs; the fixture shows that the check fires, when,
and what it says. The parameter
"sync_in_tail" (default no) schedules TestSubcycleStepping_SyncInTail, an
empty global-mode routine with "SYNC: tail_context", in CCTK_ANALYSIS.
par/sync_in_tail.par is test/subcycle_3levels.par with sync_in_tail = yes. It
lives in par/, not test/, because the testsuite has no expected-failure mode,
so it is run by hand, from a scratch directory:

  ! $CACTUS/exe/cactus_<config> <repo>/TestSubcycleStepping/par/sync_in_tail.par \
      > sync_in_tail.log 2>&1 &&
    grep -F 'TestSubcycleStepping::TestSubcycleStepping_SyncInTail (CCTK_ANALYSIS) requested SYNC of TESTSUBCYCLESTEPPING::TAIL_CONTEXT at iteration 1' sync_in_tail.log

The SYNC in initialization's CCTK_ANALYSIS passes, since that window holds
every level. The run must exit with an error in iteration 1's CCTK_ANALYSIS,
whose window holds only level 2, with this message (the flesh spells group
names in upper case):

  TestSubcycleStepping::TestSubcycleStepping_SyncInTail (CCTK_ANALYSIS)
  requested SYNC of TESTSUBCYCLESTEPPING::TAIL_CONTEXT at iteration 1 with
  CarpetX::use_subcycling = yes, while the end-of-step window holds only
  levels [2,3). The sync would fill the coarse-fine ghosts of level 2 from
  level 1, which has stepped ahead and has not been finalized. Under
  subcycling CCTK_POSTSTEP, CCTK_ANALYSIS, CCTK_CHECKPOINT and CCTK_TERMINATE
  may sync only when the end-of-step window holds every level. Otherwise
  remove the SYNC: write pointwise outputs everywhere; outputs that need a
  stencil stay interior and their ghosts are invalid. See "End-of-step bins
  under subcycling" in the CarpetX documentation.
